1,Oto-Manguean languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oto-Manguean_languages,"The highest number of speakers of Oto-Manguean languages today is in the Mexican state of Oaxaca, where the two largest branches, the Zapotecan and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined.","Zapotec, Yautepec",Otomanguean
2,San Bartolo Yautepec Zapotec - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Bartolo_Yautepec_Zapotec,"May 11, 2025 - San Bartolo Yautepec Zapotec is an Oto-Manguean language of western Oaxaca, Mexico. It is a divergent Zapotec language, 10% intelligible with Tlacolulita Zapotec and not at all intelligible with other varieties.","Zapotec, Yautepec",Otomanguean
3,"Zapotec, Yautepec Language (ZPB) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/zpb/,Yautepec Zapotec is an endangered indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family and is part of the Zapotec macrolanguage. The language is used as a first language by adults only. It is not known to be taught in schools.,"Zapotec, Yautepec",Otomanguean
4,"Zapotec - Zapotec Song - YouTubeSacred Zapotec Chants | Monte Albán Ritual Music of the Stone ...Oto-Manguean languages - WikipediaMusic and Meaning in Three Zapotec Songs * - DocsLibZapotec - Listen to music - SoundCloudZapotec, Yautepec Language (ZPB) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status ...Zapotec languages - Wikipedia",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0wDW70kUMII,"Sep 26, 2021 · ll: I yeh I yeh I yeh I yeh, I yeh I yeh I yeh om I yeh I yeh I yeh I yeh, I yeh I yeh I yeh om. :ll ll: A per tul yu tu ra me ri mi a mo a mo the mo Oe met di a mi o ka ri mi shi mi lo o way tu a.... Zapotec ritual music, ancient chants, and sacred drums from Monte Albán. Immerse yourself in ethereal voices, turtle shell percussion, bone flutes, and clay whistles echoing through the temples... The highest number of speakers of Oto-Manguean languages today is in the Mexican state of Oaxaca, where the two largest branches, the Zapotecan and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined. I start by explaining my personal relationship with this project, and how I became interested in Zapotec and in music in Zapotec. I then delve into the deepest level of my study of meaning in Ángeles’s music. Stream Zapotec, a playlist by SoundCloud from desktop or your mobile device Yautepec Zapotec is an endangered indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family and is part of the Zapotec macrolanguage. The language is used as a first language by adults only. It is not known to be taught in schools. Zapotec languages are tonal, as are Otomanguean languages generally. Unfortunately, materials on Zapotec languages vary widely in the quality of their tonal description and analysis. Are Zapotec languages related to Oto-Manguean languages? Zapotec languages (along with all Oto-Manguean languages) form part of the Mesoamerican Linguistic Area, an area of linguistic convergence developed throughout millennia of interaction between the peoples of Mesoamerica. As a result, languages have acquired characteristics from genetically unrelated languages of the area. Are Zapotec languages tonal? Zapotec languages are tonal, as are Otomanguean languages generally. Unfortunately, materials on Zapotec languages vary widely in the quality of their tonal description and analysis. Whistling has been used by Zapotec people to communicate in a way that would not be detected by their colonizers. Where are the Zapotec languages spoken? Small populations in California and New Jersey, United States. The Zapotec / ˈzæpətɛk / ZAP-ə-tek languages are a group of around 50 closely related indigenous Mesoamerican languages that constitute a main branch of the Oto-Manguean language family and are spoken by the Zapotec people from the southwestern-central highlands of Mexico. Are Zapotec and Chatino the same language? Zapotec and the related Chatino languages together form the Zapotecan subgroup of the Oto-Manguean language family. Zapotec languages (along with all Oto-Manguean languages) form part of the Mesoamerican Linguistic Area, an area of linguistic convergence developed throughout millennia of interaction between the peoples of Mesoamerica.","Zapotec, Yautepec",Otomanguean
5,"Sacred Zapotec Chants | Monte Albán Ritual Music of the Stone ...Oto-Manguean languages - WikipediaMusic and Meaning in Three Zapotec Songs * - DocsLibZapotec - Listen to music - SoundCloudZapotec, Yautepec Language (ZPB) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status ...Zapotec languages - Wikipedia",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KuXJg-SxfPk,"Zapotec ritual music, ancient chants, and sacred drums from Monte Albán. Immerse yourself in ethereal voices, turtle shell percussion, bone flutes, and clay whistles echoing through the temples... The highest number of speakers of Oto-Manguean languages today is in the Mexican state of Oaxaca, where the two largest branches, the Zapotecan and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined. I start by explaining my personal relationship with this project, and how I became interested in Zapotec and in music in Zapotec. I then delve into the deepest level of my study of meaning in Ángeles’s music. Stream Zapotec, a playlist by SoundCloud from desktop or your mobile device Yautepec Zapotec is an endangered indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family and is part of the Zapotec macrolanguage. The language is used as a first language by adults only. It is not known to be taught in schools. Zapotec languages are tonal, as are Otomanguean languages generally. Unfortunately, materials on Zapotec languages vary widely in the quality of their tonal description and analysis. Are Zapotec languages related to Oto-Manguean languages? Zapotec languages (along with all Oto-Manguean languages) form part of the Mesoamerican Linguistic Area, an area of linguistic convergence developed throughout millennia of interaction between the peoples of Mesoamerica. As a result, languages have acquired characteristics from genetically unrelated languages of the area. Are Zapotec languages tonal? Zapotec languages are tonal, as are Otomanguean languages generally. Unfortunately, materials on Zapotec languages vary widely in the quality of their tonal description and analysis. Whistling has been used by Zapotec people to communicate in a way that would not be detected by their colonizers. Where are the Zapotec languages spoken? Small populations in California and New Jersey, United States. The Zapotec / ˈzæpətɛk / ZAP-ə-tek languages are a group of around 50 closely related indigenous Mesoamerican languages that constitute a main branch of the Oto-Manguean language family and are spoken by the Zapotec people from the southwestern-central highlands of Mexico. Are Zapotec and Chatino the same language? Zapotec and the related Chatino languages together form the Zapotecan subgroup of the Oto-Manguean language family. Zapotec languages (along with all Oto-Manguean languages) form part of the Mesoamerican Linguistic Area, an area of linguistic convergence developed throughout millennia of interaction between the peoples of Mesoamerica.","Zapotec, Yautepec",Otomanguean
